# .gitleaksignore -- historical findings gitleaks must not re-report.
#
# Every entry is COMMIT-scoped (`<40-hex commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line>`), so it
# silences that one historical commit and nothing a later commit adds. A
# file-scoped entry (`<file>:<rule>:<line>`) would also hide any FUTURE secret
# written at that line, so none is allowed here, and every entry sits under a
# comment naming its class (src/tests/regression/test_gitleaks_config.py
# enforces both).
#
# Only push / pull_request scans are commit-ranged; a workflow_dispatch run of
# ci.yml scans every commit reachable from every remote branch and tag. These
# entries are what that scan finds once .gitleaks.toml loads the default
# ruleset, measured 2026-10-25 with gitleaks 8.24.3 (juniper-ml's
# util/ad-hoc/2026-10-10_gitleaks_probe.py `scan --scope ci-dispatch`, driven by
# util/ad-hoc/2026-10-25_gitleaks_probe_for_repo.py: 84 remote head/tag SHAs,
# 1575 commits, 18 findings) and classified with
# util/ad-hoc/2026-10-10_gitleaks_finding_inspect.py (shape and masked context
# only; no value was printed). Values are never written here.
#
# The config's [allowlist].paths (conda environment YAML, conf/*.lock,
# notes/mcp/) predates this file and is not repeated here.
#
# ============================================================================
# gitleaks' default ruleset -- every entry below classified as a false positive
